      "stageRecord": "The Bulgarian sentence is an interrogative perfect clause meaning 'Who has bought what?'. In this Government and Binding analysis, the subject is generated in Spec-IP, without a separate VP-internal subject position. The participle купил heads V′ and takes the interrogative pronominal DP какво as its complement. The predicate assigns its external Agent role to the subject DP Кой and its internal Theme role to какво. Finite I, realized as е, licenses nominative Case on Кой; V governs its complement and assigns accusative Case to какво. This is the standard-language subject–object analysis: Кой is the nominative personal interrogative, whereas an accusative personal interrogative would normally be кого. The finite auxiliary selects the participial VP and establishes the perfect construction; е is third-person singular, and купил has masculine singular participial agreement with the subject. A silent interrogative C heads CP and takes IP as its complement. The two interrogative DPs are still in their argument positions, so their surface indices identify lexical tokens but do not yet represent their final order. The auxiliary is generated in I; neither auxiliary inversion nor participial movement is assumed.",
